## Making a video

1. **Pick the subject.** Never repeat one from `{{ here }}completed videos/production-log.md`. Take the next unused idea in
   the bible's slate, or research a new one (`docs/RESEARCH.md`).
2. **Start the episode:** `{{ py }} -m studio{{ pflag }} new-episode <slug>` makes `{{ here }}episodes/epNN_<slug>/` with an
   `episode.json` and a `facts.md` to fill in.
{% if twod %}
3. **Script and facts:** `title`, `script` (the beats the voice reads, each with an `id`), `cta`, `hashtags`, the
   sound cues. Check every number; write it with its source in `facts.md`. Aim for the channel's length: about 2.6
   spoken words a second at the voice's speed.
4. **Voice it:** `{{ py }} pipeline{{ sep }}make_episode.py {{ episode }} --steps build` speaks the script and times every
   word (`build/voice/timeline.json`; `"duration": "auto"` follows the voice). Each beat's time is printed: the
   scene keys off them, so voice first, then draw.
5. **Draw the scene:** write `scene.js` (`docs/2D.md`), then `--steps stills --stills {{ stills }}` and **look at
   every still** in `build/stills/` (captions included). Fix and repeat until each one clears `docs/QUALITY.md`.
   Stills take seconds: iterate freely.
6. **Render:** `--steps render` draws every frame (a few minutes). It redraws everything by itself when the scene,
   the script or the spec's captions change.

{% else %}
3. **Script and facts:** `title`, `lines`, `hud`, `cta`, `hashtags`, the sound cue sheet. Check every number;
   write it with its source and the second it becomes true in `facts.md`. No line appears before its fact is true.
4. **Build the scene:** `{{ py }} pipeline{{ sep }}make_episode.py {{ episode }} --steps build,stills --stills {{ stills }}`
   and **look at every still** in `build/stills/`. Fix and repeat until each one clears `docs/QUALITY.md`. New
   scenes go in new modules (`pipeline/blender/worlds/<name>.py`, `effects/<name>.py`); never change a module
   another episode uses in a way that changes that episode.
5. **Check the text** without a full render: `{{ py }} pipeline{{ sep }}post{{ sep }}compose.py {{ episode }} --preview {{ preview }}`.
6. **Render:** `--steps render` (about 15 minutes to an hour on a fast graphics card; run it in the background). It
   says how far it is and carries on where a stopped render left off. If the scene was built again since the frames
   were rendered, it stops and asks: `--rerender all`, `--rerender 12-15.5` for one shot, or `--keep-frames` when the
   picture did not change. A re-render that is stopped carries on too when the same command is run again.
   **Nothing needs deleting by hand.**{% if codex %} If your shell stops a long
   command, run it again: it carries on where it stopped.{% endif %}

{% endif %}
7. **Post-production:** `--steps post` (sound, video, cover, captions, then QA -> `build/qa/report.md`). It stops on
   any FAIL. A WARN in the sound checks is a reason to fix the mix, not to ship.
8. **Your review:** look at every `build/qa/sheet_*.jpg`, the cover, and full-resolution frames from the final mp4
   at every line and big moment; read both loudness lines in the report; re-check `facts.md`. Write
   `build/qa/review.md` (picture, sound, facts) ending with `APPROVED` or `REJECTED: why`. If you're not sure it's
   right, it isn't done.
9. **Ship:** `--steps ship` copies the video to `{{ here }}completed videos/<id> - <title>/` (only if QA passed on this exact
   file and your review says APPROVED), deletes the render frames, and **checks that ReelForge's Publish tab lists it**
   with the right caption and hashtags. A video is done only when that check says READY.
10. **Log it:** add a row to `{{ here }}completed videos/production-log.md`.
11. **Tell {{ who }}** it's ready in ReelForge -> **Publish**: the title, the length, anything to know.

## Delivering to ReelForge's Publish tab

ReelForge's Publish tab (`{{ app }}{{ pflag }} publish`) lists every `{{ here }}episodes/<id>/final/<id>.mp4`, newest first, with the post pre-filled from
`episode.json`'s `title` and the hashtag line of `final/caption.txt`. {{ Who }} posts from there, by hand (each
platform's button opens its upload page and copies exactly the text that site needs) or with the browser extension
or YouTube's API, if they set one up. Either way it starts with their press, never yours.
- Keep that layout: `{{ here }}episodes/<id>/episode.json`, `final/<id>.mp4`, `final/caption.txt`, `final/cover.jpg`.
- `{{ py }} -m studio publish-check {{ episode }}` runs the same check as `ship` at any time and prints
  each platform's post.
- Never upload or post yourself, and never open the platforms' upload pages.

